2,3 $ Terricolous species. Spores bigger than 9 x 6 µm, often more or less citriform or with a papillate top. Cheilocystidia mostly cylindrical or clavate, sometimes narrowly lageniform (Hebelomoid species)
2,4 $ Lignicolous species. Spores smaller than 9 x 6 µm, usually amygdaliform or ellipsoid and not citriform. Cheilocystidia mostly narrowly lecythiform, except in H. microspora, where they are fusoid to cylindrical (Gymnopiloid species)

3,0 $ Cap 57 cm broad. Spores 911 (12) µm long. Italy.$ H. mediterranea
3,0 $ Cap 2.54 cm broad. Spores 1115 µm long. Algeria and Baltic countries $ H. domardiana

4,0 $ Spores amyloid. Cheilocystidia bigger than 40 x 9 µm, mostly fusoid to cylindrical. On pine wood. May and December. Italy $ H. microspora
4,5 $ Spores dextrinoid. Cheilocystidia smaller than 40 x 9 µm, mostly narrowly lecythiform. On coniferous wood or on Eucalyptus. SeptemberJanuary. Europe

5,0 $ Spores (3.5) 4.05.0 (5.2) µm wide. Carpophores whitish, becoming pale ochraceousalutaceous or incarnate. On coniferous wood, rarely on Salix. (September) OctoberNovember. Europe. $ H. neerlandica
5,0 $ Spores 5.26.0 (6.9) µm wide. Carpophores whitish and remaining so. On Eucalyptus wood. DecemberJanuary. Italy $ H. pallida